capability tls/available  whether this machine has TLS at all
  heroes  tls.is_available
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  ssl.HAS_TLSv1_2, ssl.HAS_TLSv1_3, ssl.OPENSSL_VERSION, ssl.OPENSSL_VERSION_INFO, ssl.OPENSSL_VERSION_NUMBER
  note    Go and Rust's libraries are linked into the program and always there; Python asks its OpenSSL. Here only a Linux machine with no `libssl.so.3` answers false. The Rust entries of this ledger are `native-tls` 0.2's and `rustls` 0.23's public names as their documentation gives them: neither crate is on this machine, so no command listed them (the census's one unrun list)

capability tls/connect  a TLS connection made to a host by name, its certificate checked for authority, dates and host
  heroes  tls.connect
  go      tls.Dial, tls.DialWithDialer, tls.Dialer, tls.Dialer.Dial, tls.Dialer.DialContext, tls.Config.ServerName, tls.Conn.VerifyHostname
  rust    native_tls::TlsConnector::new, TlsConnector::builder, TlsConnectorBuilder::build, TlsConnector::connect, TlsConnectorBuilder::use_sni, rustls::ClientConfig::builder, ClientConnection::new, StreamOwned::new, pki_types::ServerName
  python  ssl.create_default_context, ssl.SSLContext.wrap_socket (server_hostname), ssl.SSLContext.check_hostname, ssl.CERT_REQUIRED, ssl.HAS_SNI, ssl.SSLContext.verify_mode
  note    The handshake is made by the call, within the one deadline that bounds the connection too (Go's `Dialer.Timeout`). SNI is sent for a name and never for an address (RFC 6066), as all three do; an address is checked against the certificate's IP names, measured on the three platforms. A read answers its end where the peer closes with or without close_notify, as Python's `suppress_ragged_eofs=True` does by default; Go answers `io.ErrUnexpectedEOF` there

capability tls/client-over  TLS begun as the client over a stream already connected (Postgres's sslmode, a proxy's tunnel, STARTTLS)
  heroes  tls.client_over
  go      tls.Client, tls.Conn
  rust    native_tls::TlsConnector::connect (any Read + Write), rustls::StreamOwned, rustls::Stream, Connection::read_tls, Connection::write_tls, Connection::process_new_packets
  python  ssl.SSLContext.wrap_socket, ssl.SSLContext.wrap_bio, ssl.MemoryBIO, ssl.SSLObject, ssl.SSLSocket.unwrap
  note    Fails `unsupported` on macOS: the system's one API for TLS over a stream the program holds is Secure Transport, deprecated since macOS 10.15, and Network.framework makes its own socket. Rust's native-tls uses Secure Transport there still; the author's choice of 2026-11-16 was nothing deprecated. Going back to plain text after TLS (`unwrap`, STARTTLS's end) is not offered: no protocol of this library needs it. On macOS a plaintext exchange before TLS inside the one connection `tls` makes is measured possible, `tls/preamble` below

capability tls/preamble  a plaintext exchange before TLS inside a connection tls makes itself: Postgres's SSLRequest, STARTTLS of SMTP and IMAP, a proxy's CONNECT
  heroes  tls.connect_after
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    The three libraries do it with TLS over a stream the program holds (`tls/client-over`), which macOS refuses; here one call runs a list of steps, send, expect exactly, expect a line by its beginning (SMTP's `250-` continuations read past), on every platform: on macOS by an `nw_framer` between TCP and TLS inside the one Network.framework connection, elsewhere on the socket before TLS starts over it. No byte of TLS leaves before the last step, none of the peer's past it is read as the exchange's; a line is read up to 4096 bytes and a step up to 65536, and a step not met fails `invalid` with the bytes expected and heard, escaped (`step 2: expected "S", heard "N"`). `tls.listen_after` is the server's side. Run on macOS and Windows (the package's tests, plain and sanitized) and Linux arm64 (the C probe, plain and under ASan)
capability tls/listen  a socket listening for TLS connections, presenting a certificate chain and proving its key
  heroes  tls.listen
  go      tls.Listen, tls.NewListener, tls.Config.Certificates
  rust    native_tls::TlsAcceptor::new, TlsAcceptor::builder, TlsAcceptorBuilder::build, rustls::ServerConfig::builder, ServerConfig::with_single_cert
  python  ssl.SSLContext (PROTOCOL_TLS_SERVER), ssl.SSLContext.wrap_socket (server_side), ssl.Purpose.CLIENT_AUTH
  note    On Windows SChannel takes a server's key only from the key store (measured 2026-11-16: an ephemeral key is `SEC_E_NO_CREDENTIALS`), so the key is stored under a name of its own while the listener lives and removed at its close

capability tls/accept  the next TLS connection a listener holds, its handshake ended within a time
  heroes  tls.accept
  go      tls.Server, tls.Conn.Handshake (server side)
  rust    native_tls::TlsAcceptor::accept, rustls::ServerConnection::new
  python  ssl.SSLSocket.accept
  note    A client whose handshake fails is closed and its failure answered, and the caller accepts again, as Go's listener answers it at the first read

capability tls/server-over  TLS begun as the server over a stream already connected
