  python  -
  note    Only native-tls takes it; Windows and macOS read it natively, OpenSSL by its own call. Not written: a PEM key does what it does

capability tls/alpn  application protocols offered and taken, and the one chosen
  heroes  tls.protocol_of
  go      tls.Config.NextProtos, tls.ConnectionState.NegotiatedProtocol, tls.ConnectionState.NegotiatedProtocolIsMutual
  rust    native_tls::TlsConnectorBuilder::request_alpns, native_tls::TlsStream::negotiated_alpn, rustls::ClientConfig::alpn_protocols, ServerConfig::alpn_protocols, Connection::alpn_protocol
  python  ssl.SSLContext.set_alpn_protocols, ssl.SSLSocket.selected_alpn_protocol, ssl.HAS_ALPN
  note    Offered through `Client.protocols`, taken through `Server.protocols`. A server takes the first of its own list the client offers, and where none, answers without one, as Python's OpenSSL does; Go's server refuses the handshake there since Go 1.17, which SChannel and Network.framework do not do alike

