capability tls/deadline  a deadline on a TLS connection's reads and writes once it is made, and on a listener's wait for a client
  heroes  tls.set_deadline
  go      tls.Conn.SetDeadline, tls.Conn.SetReadDeadline, tls.Conn.SetWriteDeadline
  rust    native_tls::TlsStream::get_ref, native_tls::TlsStream::get_mut (the stream under it)
  python  ssl.SSLSocket.settimeout (the socket's)
  note    A time, not a span, as `net.set_deadline`'s, moving a read already waiting (on macOS within the 50 ms a wait looks again); `tls.set_accept_deadline` bounds `accept`'s wait for a client, and `accept`'s own time the handshake. A read the deadline ended loses nothing; a write it ended may have sent part of a record, and every later write fails, as Go rules of its own TLS: one call sets both sides, where Go has three, since a write's end leaves the stream good only for closing anyway. Run on macOS and Windows (the package's tests, plain and sanitized) and Linux arm64 (the C probe, plain and under ASan)
